\subsubsection{trame-vtk \cref{repo:trame-vtk} update precommit hooks and make
linter pass}

\cref{pr:Kitware/trame-vtk/108} updates trame-vtk precommit \cite{precommit} to
match the one from trame-cookiecutter \cref{repo:trame-cookiecutter}.
\citev{Ruff} is a Python linter, it can spot stylistic issues and bad practices
in Python code. Having a standard for style and best practice is very important
in collaborative code development as it makes it a lot easier to understand and
review other's code. The lint rules in the new precommit are quite strict so
many code practice and style needed to be fixed. These linter rules come from
different linters and in this repository they can come from \citev{pylint} (rule
code PLC), \citev{pycodestyle} (rule code E or W) or \citev{flake8} (rule code F
or SIM).  Here are some examples of rules 

\begin{itemize}

\item unused-import (F401): Checks for unused imports.

\item print (T201): Checks for print statements.

\item multiple-with-statements (SIM117): Checks for the unnecessary nesting of
multiple consecutive context managers.

\item import-outside-top-level (PLC0415): Checks for import statements outside
of a module's top-level scope, such as within a function or class definition.

\end{itemize}

In some cases these rules had to be ignored (like the PLC0415 if we want to
import some module only in some condition) and this could be done with the "\#
noqa: [error code]" comment.